Thanks for clarifying, Holger, I misunderstood what you were asking about.  For the problem of multiple pairwise comparisons that you're asking about, yes, it seems to me that releveling would be the right way to do things.  You could do it by brute force (passing different contrasts into different runs of lmer()), but in principle it seems to me that all the information you need to compute all the pairwise tests should be contained within a single ML model fit.  However, one would have to work out exactly how to use that information to construct all the relevant pairwise t statistics.  Perhaps this is something that would be useful to post to R-sig-ME about?

I only have passing familiarity with Tukey's HSD for ANOVA, but let me ask a basic question about its application in mixed model ANOVA: if there are violations of sphericity, won't that be very bad for standard HSD computations, because they assume the same error term in the denominator for all pairwise tests?  (Of course, violations of sphericity are bad for mixed model ANOVA in general, but for the omnibus F statistic at least you're getting contribution from all the conditions to both the numerator and denominator.)

Best

Roger

